#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120711} :activity/id #uuid "8313edbb-dabc-5be6-9269-6a1622b71d3d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049436 :topic/id #uuid "f5218ca7-a9d3-5ad9-b17a-ee16b6e2b34a" :topic/math-academy-id 1805} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121049 :tutorial/content "Suppose we know that a plane $\\Pi$ passes through the point $P(1,1,2)$ and that the vector $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 0,0,3 \\rangle$ is perpendicular to the plane. Let's write down an equation of this plane using the dot product.\n\n![](images/0f/0f8353480099369ad92701d1799f72d46eab4bbe5c17add8cb37a4f34074311b.png)\n\nWe start by considering an arbitrary point $R(x,y,z)$ that lies on the plane. The position vectors of the points $R$ and $P$ are $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 1,1,2 \\rangle$ respectively. So, the vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =r-⟨1,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis parallel to the plane. Therefore, $\\overrightarrow{PR} \\perp \\mathbf{n},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/28/28b6770bd681257b1030679a421b80e3f3d1e8fbb86d2060b64c7301d0878c5c.png)\n\nNow, recall that if two nonzero vectors are perpendicular, then their dot product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PR}\\perp \\mathbf{n} \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\overrightarrow{PR}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is called the **vector equation of the plane** that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}.$ In our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r} - \\langle 1,1,2 \\rangle) \\cdot \\langle 0,0,3 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Using the properties of the dot product, we can arrive at an alternative vector equation given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot n & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c = \\mathbf{p} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}$ is a (scalar) constant." :tutorial/id #uuid "77d83d72-9202-50d7-af28-72062f00bfa5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3255 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cf165cc4-ae6c-564d-b8e6-051c7aba1faa" :step/math-academy-id 17917 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120712}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120712 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119829 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119640 :question/id #uuid "ba897e22-8104-5772-a0d3-9b062f3592f1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4932"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f5c9c30-1745-5986-b116-e9e70a7c6161" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392994}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of the Plane Perpendicular to a Given Vector"} :step/id #uuid "e689c57c-a11c-582d-bed2-1b5777ad003d" :step/math-academy-id 17918 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120870}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119830 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119641 :question/id #uuid "a33f6c43-4e25-5d67-9f73-ca99a18fb434" :question/math-academy-id "e-4933"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0d93156-f9be-5e6e-976a-bef30dc892ee" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393029}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Is Parallel To a Plane"} :step/id #uuid "36217cb6-2731-5a89-84ce-0738c511b417" :step/math-academy-id 17919 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120714}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119831 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119642 :question/id #uuid "43586a83-879c-5caf-9063-a8f4afbb8b4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4935"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45cdbb0d-2986-5c05-8d23-dce29363051b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048729}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393043}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of the Plane Perpendicular to a Given Straight Line"} :step/id #uuid "1b13bc4d-57be-5d17-841a-4f2645bc3982" :step/math-academy-id 17921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120870 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121120 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a plane with the following normal vector: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -1,2,1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nLet's use the scalar product to find a vector that is parallel to this plane.\n\nWe denote our parallel vector as $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle t_1,t_2,t_3 \\rangle.$ Now, since $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{n},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,1⟩ & =0 \\\\ -{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}+{t}_{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find a nonzero solution to this equation. To do that, we solve the equation for *one* of the variables (it doesn't matter which one). For example, let's solve our equation for the variable $t_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =2{t}_{2}+{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the variable $t_1$ depends on $t_2$ and $t_3,$ which, in turn, can be *any* real numbers. The variables $t_2$ and $t_3$ are called **free variables**.\n\nHence, the general form of our vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 2t_2+t_3, \\, t_2, \\, t_3 \\rangle,\n$$\n\nwhere $t_2, t_3 \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTo find a particular vector parallel to the plane, we can substitute any values for $t_2$ and $t_3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- For example, setting $t_2=0$ and $t_3=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1= \\langle 1, 0, 1 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Or, we could set $t_2=1$ and $t_3=0$ and get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\langle 2, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n**Note:** There are infinitely many vectors parallel to a given plane. In our example, we found only two particular vectors." :tutorial/id #uuid "980ef6f4-542d-5915-84b8-e34f4480ae68" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6411 :tutorial/title "A Vector Parallel To a Plane"} :step/id #uuid "69d22458-d2d5-5c3d-b6fb-4714f2ea1b49" :step/math-academy-id 38733 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120713}}] :activity/title "The Vector Equation of a Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120016}]}
